## Root Cause Analysis & Prescribed Fix (AUTHORITATIVE — start here, do not re-trace)
A code investigation already traced this bug to its cause and prescribed the minimal fix below. This is the plan of record. Apply it; do NOT re-read the whole codebase to re-derive it.

The Acceptance Criteria above describe the desired END BEHAVIOR to VERIFY — they are NOT an implementation blueprint. Do not re-architect, split values, or add new fields/abstractions to satisfy an AC literally when the prescribed minimal fix already makes that AC pass. Implement the fix below; the ACs are how you check you got it right.

HARD RULES:
- Make the SMALLEST change that fixes the root cause. Fewer lines of code is always better.
- REUSE existing functions. Before writing any new helper, search the repo for an existing util/parser/formatter that already does what you need (use the CodeGraph tool documented below) and call it. Writing novel code when a helper already exists is a defect to be rejected in review.
- **src/context/ContentstackContext.tsx** (`ContentstackProvider`): This is the single point where all Contentstack content enters the React tree. Every component (236+ callers of useContent) reads content via useContentstackContext which consumes this provider's value. The provider currently computes content once via useMemo from defaultContent (static page props) and never updates it — making it impossible for live preview edits to propagate. This is the CAUSE: content is frozen at build-time, not merely displayed incorrectly.
  - **Minimal fix:** Replace the useMemo with useState initialized from defaultContent, and add a useEffect that subscribes to Contentstack Live Preview SDK's onContentUpdate callback to call the state setter when content changes. Specifically: change `const context = useMemo(() => ({ content: defaultContent }), [defaultContent])` to `const [content, setContent] = useState(defaultContent)`, add a useEffect that calls `Stack.livePreview.onContentUpdate((updatedContent) => setContent(prev => ({ ...prev, ...updatedContent })))` (using the Contentstack SDK's livePreview helper already available from the @contentstack/live-preview-utils package which the project's Contentstack SDK dependency provides). The context value becomes `{ content }` from state instead of from useMemo. This is the minimal change: one static computation → one state variable + one subscription, enabling all 236+ consuming components to re-render on live edit without any component-level changes.


## Verification Criteria (what a tester will CONFIRM — your change must satisfy every one)
These are observable checks, derived from the acceptance criteria and description. They describe WHAT is observed, not how to build it. Make the minimal change that makes all of these true; your accompanying test should assert them:
- With a page already open in the browser, a tester edits a content field in Contentstack and observes the corresponding text on the rendered page change to the new value without any manual refresh.
- After editing content in Contentstack, the tester observes the updated content appear on the page while the browser performs no full page reload and the URL in the address bar remains unchanged.
- While viewing the application in the GO Transit brand context, a tester edits a GO Transit content entry in Contentstack and observes the edited content appear on the rendered GO Transit page.
- While viewing the application in the UP Express brand context, a tester edits a UP Express content entry in Contentstack and observes the edited content appear on the rendered UP Express page.
- While viewing the application in the MX brand context, a tester edits an MX content entry in Contentstack and observes the edited content appear on the rendered MX page.
- When live preview is unavailable, the tester observes that the page displays the currently published content with no visual difference from the published site.

